From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hibreak <hibreak@localhost>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 20:47:41 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] sf: e-ink: less work per frame and per property write

Same output, less work on SurfaceFlinger's main thread and the GPU:

- Render pass shaders (checked bit-exact against the previous ones with
  skia-python on 15 corpus screens and synthetic frames, 14 uniform sets):
  the 16-level version (Default's greys in FAST, Reading's HD) reads its
  4 neighbours only where the 1-px contour can hold at all (not on paper,
  l > 1 - c0, nor on text and dark fills, l <= clight - cmax, with margins
  for half precision). All three skip pow() and the threshold map for pure
  black and white (the threshold lies strictly inside (0, 1)), and the full
  version skips the edge, stroke, contour and knockout tests on flat pixels
  (spread below t1, c0 and ms * 0.6, where all of them are off). The
  16-level output values per step are computed on the CPU (uniforms
  pv0..pv3, (16 * level + 4) / 255) instead of per pixel.
- Property caches: EinkMotion and the render pass settings reloaded ~60
  properties whenever any property changed, i.e. on every finger down and
  up, every mode change and every panel hold. EinkPropertyWatch tells the
  writes of the frequently written properties apart (each update adds 1 to
  the area serial and 2 to the property's serial): those are read directly
  every frame, the tuning properties only when something else changed.
  Checked: the new render pass settings equal the old ones over 300,000
  random property sequences.
- The keyboard state for debug.sf.eink.fast_full goes from EinkMotion to
  the render pass in-process instead of through the property
  vendor.xrz.hibreak_ime_visible (an IPC per keyboard change).
- Going to sleep from a reader's menu writes the sleep mode once instead of
  the page mode and then the sleep mode.
- EinkMotion's thread reads the touch and fingerprint properties from cached
  prop_info, wakes exactly when the touch hold runs out, and no longer
  repaints at the end of a fast mode that someone else replaced (no one-shot,
  so the panel never saw it; it only cost a composition).
- No region union for a single damage rectangle, reserved vectors for the
  client target damage, and the layer name scans stop once the keyboard and
  the status bar are found.
